※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=-4 diag=none

※ An answer that is an infinity is an answer the operation did not have, so
※ the program stops, the way it does when a sum will not fit its type.  That
※ is what keeps floating point behaving like the rest of the language rather
※ than like an exception to it: elsewhere every value a program holds is a
※ value its type can represent, and an infinity is what a type says when it
※ cannot say the number.  Four is SIGILL, which every target raises.

let huge: mut f64 = 1e308f64
let ten: mut f64 = 10f64

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    @[ignore(5005)]
    huge × ten
    0u8
